[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Design Patterns: Creational (Singleton, Factory, Builder)"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Creational design patterns in Object-Oriented Programming (OOP) deal with object creation mechanisms, trying to create objects in a manner suitable to the situation. The basic form of object creation could result in design problems or added complexity to the design. Creational patterns solve this by controlling the object creation process."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. The Singleton Pattern"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"The Singleton pattern ensures that a class has only one instance and provides a global point of access to that instance. This is particularly useful for managing shared resources like database connections, configuration settings, or logging services."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"public class DatabaseConnection {
    private static DatabaseConnection instance;

    private DatabaseConnection() {
        // Private constructor prevents direct instantiation
    }

    public static synchronized DatabaseConnection getInstance() {
        if (instance == null) {
            instance = new DatabaseConnection();
        }
        return instance;
    }
}"},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Controlled access to a sole instance."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Reduced namespace pollution by avoiding global variables."},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"2. The Factory Method Pattern"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"The Factory Method pattern defines an interface for creating an object, but lets subclasses decide which class to instantiate. Factory Method lets a class defer instantiation to subclasses, promoting loose coupling by eliminating the need to bind application-specific classes into the code."},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"public interface Document {
    void open();
}

public class PDFDocument implements Document {
    public void open() { System.out.println("Opening PDF Document"); }
}

public class WordDocument implements Document {
    public void open() { System.out.println("Opening Word Document"); }
}

public abstract class DocumentCreator {
    public abstract Document createDocument();
}

public class PDFCreator extends DocumentCreator {
    public Document createDocument() { return new PDFDocument(); }
}"},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Connects parallel class hierarchies."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Encourages the Open/Closed Principle by allowing new types to be introduced without breaking existing code."},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"3. The Builder Pattern"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"The Builder pattern separates the construction of a complex object from its representation, allowing the same construction process to create different representations. It is heavily used when an object requires numerous parameters, many of which are optional, avoiding the telescoping constructor anti-pattern."},{"order":15,"type":"code","text":"public class Computer {
    private String CPU;
    private String RAM;
    private boolean isGraphicsCardEnabled;

    private Computer(ComputerBuilder builder) {
        this.CPU = builder.CPU;
        this.RAM = builder.RAM;
        this.isGraphicsCardEnabled = builder.isGraphicsCardEnabled;
    }

    public static class ComputerBuilder {
        private String CPU;
        private String RAM;
        private boolean isGraphicsCardEnabled;

        public ComputerBuilder(String cpu, String ram) {
            this.CPU = cpu;
            this.RAM = ram;
        }

        public ComputerBuilder setGraphicsCard(boolean enabled) {
            this.isGraphicsCardEnabled = enabled;
            return this;
        }

        public Computer build() {
            return new Computer(this);
        }
    }
}"},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Allows step-by-step construction of complex objects."},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Improves readability and maintains immutability of the final object."}]
